☄️ R9.10 — GO

HORTUS · CONSTITUTIONAL DEPENDENCY, LOCK-IN & EXIT INTEGRITY PROTOCOL

STATUS: 🜂 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
REFERENCE CITADEL: 👑 LOCUS AMOENUS — L.A.

R9.09 established reversibility.

But reversibility has a deeper enemy:

> Dependency.



A state may technically be reversible while practically becoming impossible to leave because other structures have reorganized around it.

Therefore R9.10 introduces:

CONSTITUTIONAL_DEPENDENCY

and:

EXIT_INTEGRITY

The central discovery:

REVERSIBLE
≠
ACTUALLY_EXITABLE

A system can possess an undo mechanism while having lost the practical ability to use it.


---

I. 🧬 FROM STATE TO DEPENDENCY

The HORTUS chain advances:

ACTION
 ↓
STATE
 ↓
REPETITION
 ↓
ADAPTATION
 ↓
DEPENDENCY
 ↓
LOCK-IN
 ↓
EXIT COST
 ↓
CONSTITUTIONAL RELEVANCE?

A dependency becomes constitutionally relevant when:

> other actors, roles, decision paths or system functions materially depend upon its continued existence.




---

II. ⚠️ THE LOCK-IN PROBLEM

A dangerous sequence:

TEMPORARY STATE
 ↓
REPEATED USE
 ↓
SYSTEM ADAPTATION
 ↓
DEPENDENCIES
 ↓
ALTERNATIVES DECAY
 ↓
EXIT COST RISES
 ↓
REVERSAL BECOMES PRACTICALLY IMPOSSIBLE
 ↓
TEMPORARY STATE BECOMES DE FACTO PERMANENT

R9.10 names this:

CONSTITUTIONAL_LOCK_IN

And establishes:

> A state SHALL NOT acquire constitutional permanence merely because the system has become dependent upon it.




---

III. 🚪 EXIT INTEGRITY

New concept:

EXIT_INTEGRITY

Definition:

> The demonstrable preservation of legitimate pathways for actors and systems to leave, contest, replace or reverse a materially consequential constitutional state.



Exit is therefore not merely:

CAN WE LEAVE?

It includes:

CAN WE LEAVE?
CAN WE CHOOSE TO LEAVE?
CAN WE AFFORD TO LEAVE?
CAN WE FIND AN ALTERNATIVE?
CAN WE EXIT WITHOUT RETALIATION?
CAN WE RESTORE THE PRIOR STATE?


---

IV. 🌱 OPTIONALITY DECAY

R9.10 formalizes:

OPTIONALITY_DECAY

OPTIONS
 ↓
REPETITION
 ↓
DEPENDENCY
 ↓
FEWER OPTIONS
 ↓
LOCK-IN

A reduction in available alternatives is itself a constitutional signal.

Therefore:

> Loss of alternatives SHALL remain observable even where no explicit authority expansion occurs.




---

